items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Thistle Dispatch loads a courier's next stop and prints a summary.

```js
function loadStop(stopId) {
  // Pretend this talks to the network. It returns a receipt, not a stop.
  return Promise.resolve({ id: stopId, suburb: 'Ferndale' });
}

async function summarise(stopId) {
  const stop = await loadStop(stopId);   // suspend here; caller keeps going
  return `Next stop: ${stop.suburb}`;
}

async function main() {
  console.log('starting');
  const line = await summarise('s-1');   // await again, because summarise is async
  console.log(line);                     // 'Next stop: Ferndale'
}
```

Now remove the `await` inside `main`:

```js
const line = summarise('s-1');
console.log(line);  // Promise { <pending> }  — no error, just the wrong thing
```

Nothing threw. `line` is a receipt, and if you interpolate it into a template string you get the string `[object Promise]` on screen. The defect is invisible until someone reads the output.

solution.code:
function summariseSettled(results) {
  const values = [];
  let rejected = 0;
  for (const entry of results) {
    if (entry.status === 'fulfilled') {
      values.push(entry.value);
    } else {
      rejected += 1;
    }
  }
  return { fulfilled: values.length, rejected, values };
}

grading.starterCode:
function summariseSettled(results) {
  return { fulfilled: 0, rejected: 0, values: [] };
}

