items.workedExampleMarkdown:
A search box in the Thistle Dispatch console, with the ordering bug and then without it.

```js
// Broken: whichever response arrives last wins, regardless of which is newest.
async function search(term) {
  const results = await api.search(term);
  render(results);
}
```

Type `fer`, then `ferndale`. If the `fer` response is slower, `render` runs twice and the last call shows results for a term the user has already finished typing.

```js
let currentRequestId = 0;
let inFlight = null;

async function search(term) {
  const requestId = ++currentRequestId;   // claim the newest slot
  inFlight?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  inFlight = controller;

  try {
    const results = await api.search(term, { signal: controller.signal });
    if (requestId !== currentRequestId) return;   // a newer search has started
    render(results);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name === 'AbortError') return;      // we cancelled it on purpose
    if (requestId !== currentRequestId) return;
    renderError(error);
  }
}
```

There are two guards and they are not redundant. `abort` stops work we no longer want; the `requestId` check handles the response that was already on the wire when we changed our minds. Note also that the `AbortError` is filtered out before the error branch runs: a cancellation we asked for is not a failure to report to the user.

solution.code:
function resolveDisplayed(events) {
  let newestRequestId = null;
  let displayed = null;
  for (const event of events) {
    if (event.type === 'request') {
      newestRequestId = event.id;
    } else if (event.id === newestRequestId) {
      displayed = event.body;
    }
  }
  return displayed;
}

grading.starterCode:
function resolveDisplayed(events) {
  const last = events[events.length - 1];
  return last ? last.body : null;
}

